;;; reduce-arg-order.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · broken-examples
;;;
;;; CONFORMED 2026-07-25. Motoi's `reduce` was previously acc-first and
;;; seed-consuming (an R6RS fold-left shape). Per Alfred's decision it has
;;; now been conformed to SRFI-1: element-first `(f elem acc)`, with the
;;; ridentity IGNORED for a non-empty list. `(reduce - 0 (list 1 2 3))`
;;; now correctly returns 2 (was -6).
;;;
;;; This card is KEPT (CLAUDE.md § Broken-examples teach the differences)
;;; because the cross-standard trap survives conformance: SRFI-1 `reduce`
;;; IGNORES the seed for a non-empty list, whereas an R6RS/Racket left-fold
;;; folds the seed in every time. The now-broken code below is the porting
;;; mistake that STILL bites — assuming the ridentity is folded in.
;;;
;;; Provenance for the DECISION: Motoi's `reduce` is now SRFI-1 (src/base.js,
;;; conformed 2026-07-25). SRFI-1 is the latest/best list spec (CLAUDE.md
;;; ladder: R7RS-small → SRFI → Motoi-own).

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "broken-example — reduce seed convention across standards")

(broken-example :name "reduce"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "the-reduce-contract"
  :std-target "SRFI-1"
  :std-rejected ("R6RS")
  :status "conformed"
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme/reference/broken-examples/reduce-arg-order.slatl"

  ;; ─── THE BROKEN CODE (runs, surprising answer) ───
  ;; The porter assumes the seed 100 is folded in (as R6RS fold-left /
  ;; Racket foldl would do). SRFI-1 `reduce` ignores it for a non-empty
  ;; list, so the answer is 6, not 106.
  :broken-code "(display (reduce + 100 (list 1 2 3)))"
  :broken-output "6"
  :runs "yes — but the seed 100 is IGNORED (a porter expecting 106 is bitten)"

  ;; ─── WHY IT IS BROKEN ───
  :why
"SRFI-1 `reduce` is specified as `(reduce f ridentity list)` where, for a
NON-empty list, `ridentity` is IGNORED and the fold is

    (fold f (car list) (cdr list))

with f called element-first: `(f elem acc)`. So

    (reduce + 100 (list 1 2 3))  =>  (fold + 1 (list 2 3))  =>  6

The seed 100 never participates. It is only returned for the EMPTY list:

    (reduce + 100 (list))        =>  100

An R6RS `fold-left` or Racket `foldl` folds the seed in every time, which
is why a port that keeps the seed expecting 106 is silently wrong. For a
zero/identity seed the two conventions coincide (that is why the trap hides:
`(reduce + 0 …)` is correct either way)."

  ;; ─── THE FIX ───
  :fix-code "(display (fold + 100 (list 1 2 3)))"
  :fix-output "106"
  :fix-runs "yes"
  :fix-note
"If you actually want the seed folded in, use SRFI-1 `fold` (which always
uses its seed): `(fold + 100 (list 1 2 3))` => 106. Reach for `reduce` only
when the head of the list is the natural starting value and the seed is just
the empty-list fallback."

  ;; ─── THE LESSON ───
  :lesson
"`reduce` is a classic cross-standard trap alongside `fold` (see
fold-family.slatl). Motoi's `reduce` is SRFI-1: element-first `(f elem acc)`,
seed used ONLY for the empty list. Never port `reduce` between Schemes by
name alone — check (1) argument order (elem-first vs acc-first) and (2)
whether the seed is folded in for non-empty lists. When you need the seed
folded in, use `fold`, not `reduce`."

  ;; ─── THE SAME IDEA IN THREE OTHER LIBRARIES ───
  :three-other-libs (
    (:lib "SRFI-1"
     :status "reduce is (f elem acc), ridentity ignored when non-empty"
     :code "(reduce + 100 (list 1 2 3))  ; => 6   ; seed ignored, (fold + 1 '(2 3))"
     :note "Motoi's target contract (conformed 2026-07-25). Seed only used for the empty list.")
    (:lib "R6RS"
     :status "no `reduce`; `fold-left` is (proc acc elem), seed always used"
     :code "(fold-left + 100 (list 1 2 3))  ; => 106  ; ((((100 + 1) + 2) + 3)"
     :note "R6RS folds the seed in every time — the convention a bitten porter
assumes. R6RS fold-left is EXCLUDED from Motoi (see fold-family.slatl).")
    (:lib "Racket"
     :status "foldl is (proc elem acc), seed always used"
     :code "(foldl + 100 (list 1 2 3))   ; => 106  ; element-first but seed IS folded in"
     :note "Racket has no bare `reduce`; `foldl` is element-first (like SRFI-1
reduce) yet still consumes the seed — so it agrees with SRFI-1 reduce only
when the seed would have been the head anyway (e.g. an identity seed).")
  )

  :motoi-standard
"Motoi `reduce` is SRFI-1: element-first `(f elem acc)`, ridentity used only
for the empty list. Conformed 2026-07-25 (was acc-first, seed always folded).
Reference callers that used a commutative op with an identity seed were
unaffected; this card now teaches the surviving seed-convention trap."

  ;; ─── THE KIND THIS IS AN INSTANCE OF (up-pointer to the error census) ───
  ;; Nothing is thrown — the wrong answer is RETURNED. The census never sees it.
  :generalized-by ("../error-cards/silent-non-errors.slatl"))
